  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper conducts a model-size and token-scale sweep and reports validation perplexity against compute, bearing on whether loss changes with scale. It does not fit or evaluate the claimed power-law form.",
    "gate_note": "The scaling experiment reports validation-loss curves for models from 33M to 360M parameters at three token counts per model. The paper also compares cooldown and SWA schedules with cosine; those schedule comparisons do not test the claimed power-law exponents, extrapolation, or compute-optimal allocation. Downstream benchmark results compare schedules, not predictability from pretraining loss or scale. Cited scaling-law results are not recorded as findings.",
    "test_reason": "The paper uses scaling-law methodology as a tool. Its 33M–360M sweep (Fig. 12) shows that constant LR plus cooldown, and SWA, reach the same loss as cosine at matched tokens, so scaling experiments can reuse runs more cheaply. It fits no power law, reports no exponents, does no extrapolation and finds no compute-optimal allocation. The sweep's loss envelope is shown only as a figure, and the paper's own claim concerns schedule equivalence and compute savings, not the form of the scaling law. This is reporting the performance of its own method (T4), not testing V1.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Broadly correct. The authors conclude that constant LR with a short cooldown (best in 1-sqrt form) matches or beats cosine. They report that this holds across 33M–360M sweeps, a 1B model and an 8B run, and on downstream tasks. SWA gives strong but not optimal any-time models. Reusable runs cut scaling-experiment compute and GPU hours, saving about half in their setup; they estimate Chinchilla could have used under half its FLOPs. They fit no power laws.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study uses a model-size and token-count sweep with validation perplexity plotted against FLOPs, but does not fit the original scaling-law forms or derive compute-optimal exponents. It compares training schedules as its main focus.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Loss outcomes vary with learning-rate schedule, cooldown duration and form, and learning-rate choices; the paper reports these as schedule results, not as changes to fitted scaling-law exponents.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Across 33M–360M models at 10–30 tokens per parameter, final validation perplexity with 20% linear cooldown from constant LR matches cosine trained to each length (linear fit y=0.99x+0.23). SWA on constant LR falls slightly short (y=0.99x-0.30).",
        "proposed_claim": "The loss points used for scaling-law fits are insensitive to replacing per-length cosine runs with cooldowns from one constant-LR run.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
